  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "One flag chooses the depth",
      "items": [
        "Every box on the main path is an -emit value, so the compiler can be stopped and inspected at any level.",
        "-x mlir re-enters at the Toy dialect, so one stage's output can be fed back in as the next one's input."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Why the order matters",
      "items": [
        "Shape inference only works after inlining, because Toy functions stay shape-polymorphic until a call is replaced by its body.",
        "transpose(transpose(x)) folds while the op still means transpose. After lowering it is a loop nest, and the knowledge is gone."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Both exits agree",
      "items": [
        "The object path and the JIT translate the same LLVM dialect module; only what happens to the result differs.",
        "A linked prog.o prints exactly what -emit=jit prints, which is the end-to-end check that the lowering is real."
      ]
    }
  ]
